{"name":"Jojoba","hex":"#d9bd7d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jojoba","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of the species Simmondsia chinensis of shrubs native to the southwestern United States and to Mexico, the only plant known to store liquid wax in its seed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jojoba"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jojoba","blocks":[{"text":"Jojoba – also commonly called goat nut, deer nut, pignut, wild hazel, quinine nut, coffeeberry, and gray box bush – is an evergreen, dioecious shrub native to the Southwestern United States and northern Mexico. Simmondsia chinensis is the sole species of the family Simmondsiaceae, placed in the order Caryophyllales. The name \"jojoba\" is a hispanicization from hohohwi in Oʼodham, not to be confused with the almost homographic but unrelated \"jujube\" commonly grown in China."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Jojoba1343.JPG/330px-Jojoba1343.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jojoba","wikidata":"Q267749"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Jojoba-weibliche-bl%C3%BCte.JPG/250px-Jojoba-weibliche-bl%C3%BCte.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Jojoba-weibliche-bl%C3%BCte.JPG/500px-Jojoba-weibliche-bl%C3%BCte.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Jojoba-weibliche-bl%C3%BCte.JPG/250px-Jojoba-weibliche-bl%C3%BCte.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJojoba-weibliche-bl%C3%BCte.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Simmondsia_chinensis_male_flower.jpg/250px-Simmondsia_chinensis_male_flowe